 succeeded in 0ms:
   260	If $\cX$ is clear from the context, we will simply speak of (identifiable) Waring subspaces and Waring identifiable subspaces (or sets of subspaces). The definition of Waring identifiability can also be formulated in terms of the $\cX$-rank.
   261	Recall that if $S$ is a subspace of $\PP^n(\bF)$, then its $\cX$-\emph{rank} (over $\mathbb{F}$), notation $rk_{\cX(\bF)}(S)$, is the minimum number of points of $\cX(\mathbb{F})$ needed to span a subspace of $\PP^n(\bF)$ containing $S$.
   262	It follows that a point $P$ in $\PP^n(\bF)$ is Waring identifiable if it is contained in exactly one Waring subspace of $\PP^n(\bF)$ of dimension the $\cX$-rank of $P$ minus one.
   263	When the chosen algebraic variety is the Veronese variety, this definition is equivalent to the one given earlier on, related to the theory of symmetric tensors and in particular to Waring's problem as described in the introduction.
   264	
   265	Clearly, a point of $\bP(\bF)$ is Waring identifiable (and also a Waring subspace) with respect to $\cX$ if and only if it is a point of $\cX(\bF)$.
   266	A line  $\ell$ of $\bP(\bF)$ is a Waring subspace with respect to $\cX$ if and only if $|\ell \cap \cX(\bF)|\geq 2$, and if $|\ell \cap \cX(\bF)|= 2$ then $\ell$ is also Waring identifiable. Note that a Waring subspace is not necessarily Waring identifiable; a trivial example of a Waring subspace which is not Waring identifiable is given by a trisecant to a planar cubic curve.
   267	
   268	
   269	\begin{example}\label{ex:conic}
   270	For a non-degenerate conic $\cX$ in $\PP^2_q$, all of its points are identifiable Waring subspaces.
   271	For a non-degenerate conic $\cX$ in the Fano plane $\PP^2_2$, all but one point not on $\cX$ have $\cX$-rank two and are Waring identifiable, the remaining point (which is the nuclei of $\cX$) is also Waring identifiable, but has $\cX$-rank $3$. The Waring lines are the secant lines, and they are identifiable Waring subspaces. The tangent lines and the external lines are Waring identifiable with respect to $\cX$ but are not Waring subspaces.
   272	For a non-degenerate conic $\cX$ in $\PP^2_3$, all the exterior points to $\cX$ are Waring identifiable with respect to $\cX$. They have $\cX$-rank two. The interior points are not Waring identifiable. The Waring lines are the secant lines, whereas the remaining lines are neither Waring subspaces nor Waring identifiable.
   273	For a non-degenerate conic $\cX$ in $\PP^2_q$ with $q\geq 4$, points off $\cX$ are neither Waring subspaces nor Waring identifiable. The Waring lines are the secant lines, whereas the remaining lines are neither Waring subspaces nor Waring identifiable.
   274	\end{example}
   275	
   276	
   277	
   278	We are interested in Waring (identifiable) subspaces up to the following natural equivalence relation. Let $\Aut(\cX(\bF))$ denote the group of collineations of $\bP^n(\bF)$ fixing $\cX(\bF)$. Note that $\Aut(\cX)$ is not necessarily equal to $\Aut(\cX(\bF))$, see e.g. \cite[Exercise 10.7]{Harris}. Two subspaces $S_1$ and $S_2$ of $\PP^n(\bF)$ are called 
   279	$\cX$-\emph{equivalent} (or {\it equivalent with respect to $\cX$}) if there exists an automorphism $\varphi \in \Aut(\cX(\bF))$ such that $\varphi(S_1)=S_2$. If $\varphi$ is linear, then the subspaces $S_1$ and $S_2$ are called {\it projectively equivalent} ({\it with respect to $\cX$}).
   280	
   281	
   282	
   283	We define the {\it Waring polynomial of $\cX$ over $\bF$} (or {\it $\bF$-Waring polynomial of $\cX$}) as
   284	\begin{eqnarray}\label{eqn:Waring_pol}
   285	\cW_\cX(\bF)=\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} \lambda_i(\cX(\bF)) X^i
   286	\end{eqnarray}
   287	where $\lambda_i(\cX(\bF))$ is the number of ${\mathrm{Aut}}(\cX(\bF))$-orbits of $i$-dimensional Waring subspaces of $\PP^n(\bF)$ under the action of $\Aut(\cX(\bF))$ on the set of subspaces of $\bP^n(\bF)$. Note that $\cW_\cX(\bF)$ is meaningful when $\Aut(\cX(\bF))$ has a finite number of orbits on subspaces of $\bP^n(\bF)$, a property which is obviously satisfied for $\bF=\bF_q$.
   288	
   289	The {\it Waring identifiable polynomial} of $\cX$ is defined as
   290	\begin{eqnarray}\label{eqn:Waring_pol}
   291	\cW\cI_\cX(\bF)=\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} \mu_i(\cX(\bF)) X^i
   292	\end{eqnarray}
   293	where $\mu_i(\cX(\bF))$ is the number of ${\mathrm{Aut}}(\cX(\bF))$-orbits of $i$-dimensional Waring identifiable subspaces of $\PP^n(\bF)$.
   294	
   295	
   296	The {\it identifiable Waring polynomial} of $\cX$ is defined as
   297	\begin{eqnarray}\label{eqn:Waring_pol}
   298	\cI\cW_\cX(\bF)=\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} \eta_i(\cX(\bF)) X^i
   299	\end{eqnarray}
   300	where $\eta_i(\cX(\bF))$ is the number of ${\mathrm{Aut}}(\cX(\bF))$-orbits of $i$-dimensional identifiable Waring subspaces of $\PP^n(\bF)$.
   301	
   302	
   303	\begin{example}
   304	The above defined polynomials are easily obtained for a non-degenerate conic $\cX$ in $\PP^2_q$. The analysis made in Example \ref{ex:conic} implies that for $q=2$ we have
   305	\[ \mathcal{W}_{\cX}(\bF_2)=1+X,\,\,\mathcal{WI}_{\cX}(\bF_2)=3+3X,\,\, \mathcal{IW}_{\cX}(\bF_2)=1+X; \]
   306	for $q=3$ we have
   307	\[ \mathcal{W}_{\cX}(\bF_3)=1+X,\,\,\mathcal{WI}_{\cX}(\bF_3)=2+X,\,\, \mathcal{IW}_{\cX}(\bF_3)=1+X; \]
   308	and for $q\geq 4$ we have
   309	\[ \mathcal{W}_{\cX}(\bF_q)=1+X,\,\,\mathcal{WI}_{\cX}(\bF_q)=1+X,\,\, \mathcal{IW}_{\cX}(\bF_q)=1+X. \]
   310	\end{example}
