   314	\subsection{The Veronese variety}\label{sec:Vero}
   315	
   316	Since we are interested in the Waring problem and the (symmetric) rank of tensors in ${\mathrm{Sym}}^dV$, the algebraic variety at hand is the Veronese variety, which we now define.
   317	
   318	Following \cite{Harris}, for any $n$ and $d$, we define the \emph{Veronese map of degree $d$}
   319	
   320	\begin{equation*}
   321	\begin{array}{cccc}
   322	\nu_d\colon & \PP^n & \longrightarrow & \PP^N\\
   323	& (X_0,\ldots,X_n) & \mapsto & (\ldots,X^I,\ldots)
   324	\end{array}
   325	\end{equation*}
   326	
   327	\noindent where $X^I$ ranges over all monomials of degree $d$ in $X_0,\ldots,X_n$ and $N={{n+d}\choose{d}}-1$.
   328	The image of the Veronese map is an algebraic variety called \emph{Veronese variety} and usually denoted by $\VV_{n,d}$ (or $\VV_{n,d}(\mathbb{F})$). The Veronese variety $\VV_{n,2}$ obtained for $d=2$ is also called the {\it quadric Veronesean}.
   329	If we regard $\PP^N$ as $\PP(\mathbb{F}[x_0,\ldots,x_n]_d)$, i.e.\ as $\PP(\mathrm{Sym}^d(V))$, the Veronese variety parametrizes those polynomials that can be written as $d$-th powers of a linear form, i.e. the pure elements of $\mathrm{Sym}^d(V)$.
   330	The $\VV_{n,d}$-rank of a point in $\PP(\mathrm{Sym}^d(V))$ coincides with the rank of the nonzero tensors defining the point, and a point is Waring identifiable with respect to $\VV_{n,d}$ if the nonzero tensors defining the point are Waring identifiable.
   331	
   332	The projective stabliser of $\VV_{n,d}(\mathbb{F})$ is in general obtained by lifting the action of $\PGL(n+1,\mathbb{F})$ from $\PP^n$ to $\PP^N$ through the Veronese map $\nu_d$, see e.g. \cite{Harris}, however caution should be taken when working over $\mathbb{F}=\bF_2$, in which case the projective stabiliser of $\VV_{n,d}(\mathbb{F})$ is the full symmetric group $S_7$, instead of the much smaller ${\mathrm{PSL}}(3,2)$.
   333	
   334	An important property of the Veronese map of degree $d$ is that hypersurfaces in $\bP^n$ of degree $d$ correspond to hyperplanes of $\bP^N$. Moreover, the set of $\bF$-rational points of a hypersurface of degree $d$ corresponds to the set of points in a hyperplane section of the Veronese variety $\VV_{n,d}(\bF)$. More generally, subspaces of $\bP^N$
   335	correspond to linear systems of hypersurfaces of degree $d$ on $\bP^n$. Restricting to the case $d=2$, we recall the following well-known facts:
   336	\begin{enumerate}
   337	    \item the hyperplanes sections of $\VV_{2,2}(\bF)$ in $\mathbb{P}^5$ are the image of conics in $\mathbb{P}^2$,
   338	    \item the hyperplanes sections of $\VV_{3,2}(\bF)$ in $\mathbb{P}^{9}$ are the image of quadrics in $\mathbb{P}^3$, and
   339	    \item the linear section of $\VV_{3,2}(\bF)$ with a subspace of co-dimension two in $\mathbb{P}^{9}$ is the image of the base of a pencil of quadrics in $\mathbb{P}^3$.
   340	\end{enumerate}
   341	
   342	
   343	\section{Identifiable Waring subspaces with respect to the quadric Veronesean}\label{sec:Waring}
   344	
   345	
   346	The rest of the paper is devoted to the study of properties, and to provide examples, of identifiable Waring subspaces with respect to the quadric Veronesean $\VV_{n,2}$.
   347	
   348	\smallskip
   349	
   350	We start by giving some trivial examples of identifiable Waring subspaces. First of all, since the quadric Veronesean is the intersection of quadrics and does not contain any lines, each two distinct points $P_1$ and $P_2$ on the quadric Veronesean $\VV_{n,2}$ determine a line $\langle P_1,P_2\rangle$ which meets $\VV_{n,2}$ in $P_1$ and $P_2$ only, i.e. $\langle P_1,P_2\rangle$ is an identifiable Waring line with respect to $\VV_{n,2}$.
   351	
   352	\begin{remark}\label{rk:smaller}
   353	If $\mathcal{S}=\langle P_1,\ldots,P_{k} \rangle$ is an identifiable Waring subspace, then 
   354	$$\langle P_{i_1},\ldots,P_{i_{\ell+1}} \rangle$$ 
   355	is an identifiable Waring subspace of dimension $\ell$ for any choice of $i_1,\ldots,i_{\ell+1}$ distinct elements in  $\{1,\ldots,k\}$.
   356	\end{remark}
   357	
   358	\smallskip
   359	
   360	A general construction of an identifiable Waring subspace is given by the following result, which is well-known.
   361	In the proof, and in the remainder of the paper, the vectors $\mathbf{e}_0,\ldots,\mathbf{e}_n$ denote the vectors of a basis of $V\cong\bF^{n+1}$.
   362	\begin{theorem}
   363	The subspace $\mathcal{S}$ spanned by the images of a frame in $\PP^n$ is an $(n+1)$-dimensional identifiable Waring subspace with respect to $\VV_{n,2}$.
   364	\end{theorem}
   365	\begin{proof}
   366	Without loss of generality we may assume that
   367	\[ \mathcal{S}=\left\langle \mathbf{e}_0^{\otimes2},\ldots, \mathbf{e}_n^{\otimes2}, (\mathbf{e}_0+\ldots+\mathbf{e}_{n})^{\otimes2} \right\rangle. \]
   368	Each point of $\mathcal{S}$ correspond to a matrix of the form
   369	$$
   370	M_{a_0,\ldots,a_{n+1}}:=a_{n+1}I_{n+1}+diag(a_0,\ldots,a_n),
   371	$$
   372	with $a_0,\ldots,a_{n+1} \in \bF_q$.
   373	Clearly, $\mathcal{S}$ is an identifiable Waring subspace if and only if the only case in which the matrix 
   374	$M_{a_0,\ldots,a_{n+1}}$ has rank one is when $n+1$ of the elements $a_0,\ldots,a_{n+1}$ are zero and the remaining element is nonzero. Clearly $M_{a_0,\ldots,a_{n},0}$
   375	has rank one if and only if $n$ elements in $\{a_0,\ldots,a_{n}\}$ are zero and the remaining one is non-zero.
   376	Now, if $a_{n+1}\neq0$, then we may assume that $a_{n+1}=1$ and in this case
   377	$M_{a_0,\ldots,a_{n},1}$ has rank one if and only if all its rows coincide, which happens if and only if $a_1=a_2=\ldots=a_n=0$.
   378	Hence, the assertion is proved.
   379	\end{proof}
   380	
   381	As a consequence, we have the following.
   382	
   383	\begin{corollary}
   384	If $\mathcal{S}$ is generated by the images under $\nu_{2}$ of $\ell+1$ points contained in a frame of $\PP^n$, then $\mathcal{S}$ is an $\ell$-dimensional identifiable Waring subspace with respect to $\VV_{n,2}$.
   385	\end{corollary}
   386	
   387	The above results give two types of constructions for an $\ell$-dimensional identifiable Waring subspace $\mathcal{S}$ with respect to $\VV_{n,2}$.
   388	\begin{enumerate}
   389	  \item[(S1)] The image under $\nu_{2}$ of a frame of an $\ell$-dimensional subspace in $\PP^n$.
   390	  \item[(S2)] The image under $\nu_2$ of $\ell+1$ points which are in general position in $\PP^n$.
   391	\end{enumerate}
   392	In both cases
   393	\[ \mathcal{S}=\langle \nu_{2}(P_1),\ldots,\nu_{2}(P_{\ell+1})\rangle \]
   394	is an identifiable Waring subspace with respect to $\VV_{n,2}$.
   395	
   396	
   397	\section{Identifiable Waring subspaces with respect to $\VV_{2,2}(\bF_q)$}\label{sec:2,2}
   398	
   399	Identifiable Waring planes and Waring solids of $\PP^5_q$ with respect to $\VV_{2,2}$ are easy to construct, see constructions (S1) and (S2). The next result classifies the identifiable Waring subspaces with respect to $\VV_{2,2}$ of any dimension in $\PP^5_q$.
   400	
   401	\begin{theorem}\label{thm:V_2,2}
   402	$(i)$ The identifiable Waring lines of $\bP_q^5$ with respect to $\VV_{2,2}$ are the span of the images under $\nu_2$ of any two distinct points in $\PP^2_q$.\\
   403	$(ii)$ If $q>2$, then the identifiable Waring planes are those obtained by contruction (S2) with $\ell=2$, while for $q=2$, the identifiable Waring planes are the span of the images under $\nu_2$ of any three points in $\PP_q^2$.\\
   404	$(iii)$ For $q>2$, the identifiable Waring solids are those obtained by contruction (S1) with $\ell=2$, whereas for $q=2$, the identifiable Waring solids are obtained as the span of the images of four non-collinear points in $\PP_q^2$.\\
   405	$(iv)$ The identifiable Waring hyperplanes are the images under $\nu_2$ of irreducible conics for $q=4$, and two distinct lines for $q=2$.
   406	\end{theorem}
   407	
   408	\begin{proof}
   409	$(i)$ Identifiable Waring lines. As $\VV_{2,2}$ is a the intersection of quadrics of $\PP^5$ and does not contain any lines, two distinct points of $\VV_{2,2}$ identify a $2$-secant line to $\VV_{2,2}$. Therefore, the span of the images under $\nu_2$ of any two distinct points of $\PP^2$ is an identifiable Waring line.
   410	
   411	$(ii)$ Identifiable Waring planes. Since the images under $\nu_2$ of three collinear points in $\PP_q^2$ determine a $(q+1)$-secant plane to $\VV_{2,2}(\bF_q)$, if $q>2$, identifiable Waring planes can only be obtained by contruction (S2).
   412	
   413	$(iii)$ Identifiable Waring solids. If $q>2$, an identifiable Waring subspace of dimension $3$ of $\PP_q^5$ cannot be obtained as the span of the images under $\nu_2$ of four points in $\PP_q^2$ of which (at least) three are collinear. Indeed, consider $P_1, P_2, P_3, P_4$ points in $\PP_q^2$. Clearly, if they are collinear, then $\dim \langle \nu_2(P_1), \nu_2(P_2), \nu_2(P_3), \nu_2(P_4) \rangle =2$. Assume now that $P_1, P_2, P_3$ are collinear and $P_4 \notin \langle P_1, P_2\rangle$. Then 
   414	$$|\langle \nu_2(P_1), \nu_2(P_2), \nu_2(P_3), \nu_2(P_4) \rangle \cap\VV_{2,2}(\bF_q)|\geq q+2,$$ 
   415	which is always greater than $4$.
   416	For $q=2$ the images under $\nu_2$ of any $4$ points in $\PP_q^2$ which are not collinear determine an identifiable Waring solid.
   417	
   418	$(iv)$ Identifiable Waring hyperplanes.
   419	The hyperplanes sections of $\PP^5$ are in one to one correspondence with the conics of $\PP^2$.
   420	The conics of $\PP_q^2$ have either $1$, $q+1$ (irreducible conics or double line) or $2q+1$ (two distinct lines) points.
   421	Therefore, identifiable Waring hyperplanes of $\PP_q^5$ are those arising from irreducible conics if $q=4$ and those from two distinct lines for $q=2$.
   422	\end{proof}
   423	
   424	The above result allows us to completely determine the identifiable Waring polynomial of $\mathbb{V}_{2,2}$ over $\bF_q$.
   425	
   426	\begin{corollary}
   427	The identifiable Waring polynomial of the quadric Veronesean $\VV_{2,2}$ over $\bF_q$, with $q\neq 2,4$, is 
   428	\begin{eqnarray}\label{eqn:V_2,2}
   429	 \mathcal{IW}_{\VV_{2,2}}(\bF_{q})=1+X+X^2+X^3.
   430	\end{eqnarray}
   431	For $q=2$ and $q=4$ the identifiable Waring polynomials are
   432	$$
   433	\mathcal{IW}_{\VV_{2,2}}(\bF_{2})=1+X+2X^2+2X^3+X^4, \mbox{ and } 
   434	\mathcal{IW}_{\VV_{2,2}}(\bF_{4})=1+X+X^2+X^3+X^4.
   435	$$
   436	
   437	\end{corollary}
   438	
   439	\begin{proof}
   440	The term $1+X$ (for all $q$) follows from the well-known fact that $\Aut(\VV_{2,2}(\bF_q))$ acts two-transitively on the points of $\VV_{2,2}(\bF_q)$. The term $X^2+X^3$ in the cases $q\neq 2$ follows from Theorem \ref{thm:V_2,2} and the well-known fact that $\PGL(3,q)$ acts transitively on triangles and on frames of $\bP_q^2$.
   441	Assume $q=2$.
   442	By the previous theorem the identifiable Waring planes are the span of the images under $\nu_2$ of any three points in $\PP_q^2$, so there will be two equivalence classes depending on whether these three points of $\PP_q^2$ are collinear or not. 
   443	Furthermore, by the previous result the identifiable Waring solids are obtained as the span of the images of four non-collinear points in $\PP_q^2$, and hence the two equivalence classes can be identified according to whether the four points are in general position or not.
   444	\end{proof}
   445	
   446	Note that when $q\neq 2,4$ there do not exist identifiable Waring hyperplanes with respect to $\VV_{2,2}(\bF_q)$.
   447	
   448	\begin{remark}
   449	The above results can also be recovered from the following (much more general) classification results. All lines with respect to $\VV_{2,2}(\bF_q)$ have been classified in \cite{LP} for both $q$ even and $q$ odd. Planes meeting $\VV_{2,2}(\bF_q)$ have been classified in \cite{LPS} for $q$ odd, and in \cite{AlLaPo2022} for $q$ even. The classification of solids with respect to $\VV_{2,2}(\bF_q)$ follows from the classification of lines for $q$ odd, while for $q$ even this classification was obtained in \cite{AlLa202*}. 
   450	\end{remark}
   451	\begin{remark}
   452	In terms of linear systems of conics the identifiable Waring polynomial \eqref{eqn:V_2,2} corresponds to the fact that, up to projective equivalence, there is a unique web of conics in $\bP_q^2$ with base two points, there is a unique net of conics with base three non-collinear points, and there is a unique pencil of conics with base a frame.
   453	\end{remark}
   454	
   455	
   456	\section{Identifiable Waring subspaces with respect to $\VV_{3,2}(\bF_q)$}\label{sec:3,2}
   457	
   458	The aforementioned constructions (S1) and (S2) give examples of identifiable Waring subspaces of $\bP_q^9$ of dimension one, two, three and four with respect to $\VV_{2,2}(\bF_q)$.
   459	In this section we investigate the existence of identifiable Waring subspaces of dimension at least $5$. 
   460	
   461	\subsection{Identifiable Waring subspaces of codimension $\geq 3$}
   462	We construct examples of identifiable Waring subspaces of dimension six in $\PP_q^9$ for all $q$ (and therefore also of dimension less than or equal to $6$ by Remark \ref{rk:smaller}).
   463	\begin{theorem}\label{6-Waring}
   464	The subspace
   465	\[ \mathcal{S}=\langle \mathbf{e}_1^{\otimes2},\mathbf{e}_2^{\otimes2},\mathbf{e}_3^{\otimes2},\mathbf{e}_4^{\otimes2},  \mathbf{e}^{\otimes2},(\mathbf{e}_1-\mathbf{e}_2-\mathbf{e}_3)^{\otimes2},(-\mathbf{e}_1+\mathbf{e}_2-\mathbf{e}_4)^{\otimes2} \rangle, \]
   466	where $\mathbf{e}=\mathbf{e}_1+\mathbf{e}_2+\mathbf{e}_3+\mathbf{e}_4$, is a $6$-dimensional identifiable Waring subspace of $\PP_q^9$ with respect to $\VV_{3,2}(\bF_q)$ when $q$ is odd.
   467	\end{theorem}
   468	\begin{proof}
   469	The points of $\mathcal{S}$ can be represented by the matrices
   470	\begin{eqnarray}\label{eqn:M1}
   471	 M_\alpha:=\left(
   472	\begin{array}{cccc}
   473	 a+e+f-g & e-f+g & e-f & e-g \\
   474	 e-f+g & b+e+f-g & e+f & e+g \\
   475	 e-f & e+f & c+e+f & e \\
   476	 e-g & e+g & e & d+e-g \\
   477	\end{array}
   478	\right),
   479	\end{eqnarray}
   480	with $\alpha=(a,b,c,d,e,f,g) \in \bF_q^7\setminus \{0\}$. One easily verifies that $\mathcal S$ is a 6-dimensional subspace of $\PP^9$. In order to prove that $\mathcal S$ is a Waring subspace, it suffices to show that the only matrices of rank one of the form (\ref{eqn:M1}) are obtained by setting all but one of the parameters $a,b,c,d,e,f,g$ equal to zero.
   481	For simplicity of notation we denote the $2\times 2$-minor of $M_\alpha$ corresponding to the rows $(i,j)$ and columns $(k,l)$ by $M_{(i,j)(k,l)}$.
   482	
   483	Suppose $M_\alpha$ has rank one for some choice of $\alpha=(a,b,c,d,e,f,g) \in \bF_q^7\setminus \{0\}$. Then 
   484	$$M_{(1,2)(3,4)}=-2e(f-g)=0$$ 
   485	which implies that $e=0$ or $f=g$, since $q$ is odd.
   486	
   487	\bigskip
   488	
   489	$(i)$ First suppose $e=0$. Then 
   490	$$M_{(3,4)(2,3)}=g(c+f)=0$$ 
   491	implies $g=0$ or $c+f=0$.
   492	
   493	If $g=0$ then $M_{(2,3)(1,2)}=0$ implies $bf=0$. If $f\neq 0$ and $b=0$, then $M_{(2,3)(2,3)}=0$ implies that $c=0$. Similarly by considering $M_{(1,2)(1,2)}$ and $M_{(3,4)(3,4)}$ it also follows that $a=0$ and $d=0$. So that in this case $a=b=c=d=e=g=0$ and $f\neq 0$. If $e=g=0$ and $f=0$, then the matrix $M_\alpha$ reduces to a diagonal matrix with $a,b,c,d$ on the diagonal. This matrix has rank one if and only if all but one of the diagonal elements are equal to zero. So, all but one of the parameters $a,b,c,d,e,f,g$ must be zero.
   494	
   495	If $g\neq 0$ then $c+f$ must be zero. The condition $M_{(2,3)(2,3)}=0$ implies that $f=0$, and therefore also $c=0$. By considering $M_{(1,2)(2,4)}$ and $M_{(1,2)(1,2)}$ it also follows that $b=0$ and $a=0$. Finally, $M_{(1,4)(2,4)}=0$ implies $d=0$. This concludes the case $e=0$.
   496	
   497	\bigskip
   498	
   499	$(ii)$ Now suppose $e\neq 0$. Then $f$ and $g$ must be equal. The condition $M_{(1,4)(2,3)}=0$ implies that $f=0$, and therefore also $g=0$. Then $M_{(1,2)(2,3)}=0$ implies that $b=0$ and $M_{(1,2)(1,2)}=0$ implies that $a=0$. Similar arguments show that also $c$ and $d$ must be zero. We may conclude that in the case $e\neq 0$ it follows that the only matrices of rank one of the form (\ref{eqn:M1}) are obtained by setting $a=b=c=d=f=g=0$, as was required.
   500	\end{proof}
   501	
   502	\begin{remark}
   503	Since the proof only relies on the fact that $2\ne0$, the above example not only works over finite fields, but works over any field of characteristic either zero or greater than two.
   504	\end{remark}
   505	
   506	\begin{remark}
   507	Computational results show that the above example works for $q=2$, but not for $q\in \{4,8\}$.
   508	Furthermore, if we consider the projective subspace of $\PP^9$ defined by the span
   509	\[\langle W,(\mathbf{e}_2-\mathbf{e}_3+\mathbf{e}_4)^{\otimes2}\rangle,\]
   510	where $W$ is as in Theorem \ref{6-Waring}, then it is an identifiable Waring subspace for $q=3$, but not for $q \in \{5,7,9\}$.
   511	\end{remark}
   512	
   513	The next theorems provide examples of identifiable Waring subspaces with respect to $\VV_{3,2}(\bF_q)$ for all $q\geq 4$.
   514	
   515	
   516	
   517	\begin{theorem}\label{6-Waring}
   518	Let $q\geq 4$. The subspace
   519	\[ \mathcal{S}=\langle \mathbf{e}_1^{\otimes2},\mathbf{e}_2^{\otimes2},\mathbf{e}_3^{\otimes2},\mathbf{e}_4^{\otimes2},\mathbf{e}^{\otimes2},  (\omega\mathbf{e}_1+\mathbf{e}_2+\omega\mathbf{e}_3)^{\otimes2},(\sqrt{\omega}\mathbf{e}_1+\sqrt{\omega}\mathbf{e}_2+\sqrt{\omega^3}\mathbf{e}_4)^{\otimes2}  \rangle, \]
   520	where $\mathbf{e}=\mathbf{e}_1+\mathbf{e}_2+\mathbf{e}_3+\mathbf{e}_4$, 
   521	is a $6$-dimensional identifiable Waring subspace of $\PP_q^9$ with respect to $\VV_{3,2}(\bF_q)$, for every $\omega \in\bF_q\setminus\{0,1,2\}$ which is a square in $\bF_q$.
   522	\end{theorem}
   523	\begin{proof}
   524	The points of $\mathcal{S}$ can be represented by the matrices
   525	\begin{equation}\label{eqn:M2}
   526	 M_{\alpha}:=\left(
   527	\begin{array}{cccc}
   528	 f \omega^2+g \omega+a+e & e+f \omega+g \omega & f \omega^2+e & g \omega^2+e \\
   529	 e+f \omega+g \omega & b+e+f+g \omega & e+f \omega & g \omega^2+e \\
   530	 f \omega^2+e & e+f \omega & f \omega^2+c+e & e \\
   531	 g \omega^2+e & g \omega^2+e & e & g \omega^3+d+e \\
   532	\end{array}
   533	\right),
   534	\end{equation}
   535	with $\alpha=(a,b,c,d,e,f,g) \in \bF_q^7\setminus \{0\}$. One easily verifies that $\mathcal S$ is a 6-dimensional subspace of $\PP_q^9$. As in the proof of Theorem \ref{6-Waring}, in order to prove that $\mathcal S$ is a Waring subspace, it suffices to show that the only matrices of rank one of the form \eqref{eqn:M2} are obtained by setting all but one of the parameters $a,b,c,d,e,f,g$ equal to zero.
   536	Again, we denote the $2\times 2$-minor of $M_\alpha$ corresponding to the rows $(i,j)$ and columns $(k,l)$ by $M_{(i,j)(k,l)}$.
   537	Assume that $M_{\alpha}$ has rank one for some $\alpha=(a,b,c,d,e,f,g)\in \mathbb{F}_q^7\setminus\{0\}$. Then 
   538	$$M_{(3,4)(1,2)}=f(\omega-1)\omega(e+g\omega^2)=0,$$ 
   539	which implies either $f=0$ or $e=-g\omega^2$.
   540	
   541	\bigskip
   542	
   543	(1) First suppose that $f=0$. Then $M_{(1,3)(1,2)}=ae=0$, that is either $a=0$ or $e=0$.
   544	
   545	(1.1) Let $a=0$. Then $M_{(1,2)(2,3)}=be=0$. If $b=0$, then $M_{(1,3)(1,4)}=eg\omega(1-\omega)=0$, which implies either $e=0$ or $g=0$. If $e=0$, then by $M_{(1,3)(1,3)}$, $M_{(1,4),(1,4)}$ and by $M_{(3,4)(3,4)}$ we have $cg=cd=dg=0$.
   546	Whereas, if $a=b=g=0$,
   547	\[ M_{\alpha}=\left( \begin{array}{cccc}
   548	e & e & e & e \\
   549	e & e & e & e \\
   550	e & e & c+e & e \\
   551	e & e & e & d+e
   552	\end{array} \right). \]
   553	Thus, in both cases all but one of the parameters $a,b,c,d,e,f,g$ must be zero.
   554	Assume now $a=e=0$ and $b\ne 0$. By $M_{(1,2),(1,2)}$, $M_{(2,4)(2,4)}$ and by $M_{(2,3)(2,3)}$ we get $c=d=g=0$, that is $a=c=d=e=f=0$ and $b\ne 0$.
   555	
   556	(1.2) Suppose that $e=0$ and $a\ne 0$. Then $M_{(1,2)(1,4)}=agw^2=0$, which implies $g=0$. So, $M_{\alpha}$ reduces to a diagonal matrix with $a,b,c,d$ on the diagonal. Hence, as before, $M_\alpha$ has rank one if and only if all but one of the parameters $a,b,c,d,e,f,g$ are zero.
   557	
   558	\bigskip
   559	
   560	(2) Suppose that $e=-g\omega^2$ and $f\ne 0$. Then, we have $M_{(1,3)(3,4)}=-g\omega^4(f-g)=0$, and hence either $g=0$ or $f=g$.
   561	If $f=g$, then 
   562	$$M_{(1,3)(2,4)}=g^2(\omega-2)\omega^3=0,$$ 
   563	and so $g=f=0$, which leads to a contradiction.
   564	If $g=0$, then $e=0$. By $M_{(1,2)(1,3)}$, $M_{(1,2)(1,2)}$, $M_{(1,3)(1,3)}$ and $M_{(1,4)(1,4)}$ we have $a=b=c=d=0$. Again we have that all but one of the parameters $a,b,c,d,e,f,g$ are zero.
   565	\end{proof}
   566	
   567	\begin{theorem}\label{thm:codim_three_P9}
   568	Let $\omega$ be an element in $\bF_q\setminus\{0,1,-1\}$.
   569	The subspace
   570	\[ \mathcal{S}=\langle \mathbf{e}_1^{\otimes2},\mathbf{e}_2^{\otimes2},\mathbf{e}_3^{\otimes2},\mathbf{e}_4^{\otimes2},  (\mathbf{e}_2+\mathbf{e}_3+\mathbf{e}_4)^{\otimes2},\mathbf{v},\mathbf{w} \rangle, \]
   571	with $\mathbf{v}=(\mathbf{e}_1+\omega \mathbf{e}_2+\omega \mathbf{e}_3+\omega^2\mathbf{e}_4)^{\otimes2}$ and $\mathbf{w}=(\mathbf{e}_1+\omega \mathbf{e}_2+ \mathbf{e}_3+\omega \mathbf{e}_4)^{\otimes2}$,
   572	is a $6$-dimensional identifiable Waring subspace of $\PP_q^9$ with respect to $\VV_{3,2}(\bF_q)$.
   573	\end{theorem}
   574	\begin{proof}
   575	The nonzero vectors of $\mathcal{S}$ can be represented by the matrices
   576	{\footnotesize{
   577	\begin{equation}\label{eqn:M_codim2}
   578	 M_\alpha:=\left(
   579	\begin{array}{cccc}
   580	 a+f+g & \omega f+\omega g & \omega f+g & \omega^2 f +\omega g  \\
   581	 \omega f+\omega g & b+e+\omega^2 f+\omega^2 g & e+\omega^2 f+\omega g & e+\omega^3 f +\omega^2 g \\
   582	 \omega f+g & e+\omega^2 f +\omega g & c+e+\omega^2 f+g & e+\omega^3 f +\omega g \\
   583	 \omega^2 f+\omega g  & e+\omega^3 f+\omega^2 g & e+\omega^3 f+\omega g & d+e+\omega^4 f +\omega^2 g \\
   584	\end{array}
   585	\right),
   586	\end{equation} }}
   587	with $\alpha=(a,b,c,d,e,f,g) \in \bF_q^7\setminus \{0\}$. A straightforward computation shows that $\mathcal S$ is a 6-dimensional subspace of $\PP^9$. In order to prove that $\mathcal S$ is a Waring subspace, it suffices to show that the only matrices of rank one of the form (\ref{eqn:M_codim2}) are obtained by setting all but one of the parameters $a,b,c,d,e,f,g$ equal to zero.
   588	Since
   589	$$M_{(2,3)(1,4)}=g (-1 + \omega) (e + f (-1 + \omega) \omega^2)=0,$$
   590	we have to analyze the case $g=0$ and the case $g \ne 0$ and $e=-f\omega^2(\omega-1)$.
   591	
   592	\bigskip
   593	
   594	$(i)$ Suppose $g=0$, then $M_{(2,3)(1,2)}=-bf\omega=0$, that is either $b=0$ or $f=0$.
   595	If $b=0$, then $M_{(2,4)(1,2)}=-\omega(\omega-1)ef=0$.
   596	If $e=0$, by evaluating $M_{(1,2)(1,2)}$, $M_{(1,3)(2,3)}$, $M_{(1,3)(1,3)}$, $M_{(2,3)(1,4)}$, $M_{(1,4)(1,4)}$ and $M_{(3,4)(3,4)}$ we get $af=cf=ac=df=ad=cd=0$.
   597	If $f=0$ and $e \ne 0$, by $M_{(1,2)(1,2)}=M_{(1,3)(1,3)}=M_{(1,4)(1,4)}=0$ we get $e=c=d=0$.
   598	Now, if $b \ne 0$ and $f=0$, from $M_{(1,2)(1,2)}=M_{(2,3)(2,3)}=M_{(2,4)(2,3)}=M_{(2,4)(2,4)}=0$ we get $a=c=d=e=0$.
   599	In these cases, all but one of the parameters $a,b,c,d,e,f,g$ equal to zero.
   600	
   601	
   602	\bigskip
   603	
   604	$(ii)$ Assume $g \ne 0$ and $e=-f\omega^2(\omega-1)$. The condition that 
   605	$$M_{(3,4)(1,2)}=f (-1 + \omega)^2 \omega^2 (g + f w)=0,$$ 
   606	implies that either $f=0$ or $g=-\omega f$. If $f=0$, then $e=0$ and, since $g\ne 0$, we get $a=b=c=d=0$, since $M_{(1,3)(1,2)}=M_{(2,3)(1,2)}=M_{(2,3)(1,3)}=M_{(2,3)(1,4)}=0$.
   607	Again, all but one of the parameters $a,b,c,d,e,f,g$ equal to zero, as required.
   608	Suppose now $g=-\omega f$ and $f \ne 0$.
   609	By $M_{(2,3)(1,2)}=(\omega-1)^2 \omega^3f^2=0$, we get $f=0$, a contradiction.
   610	\end{proof}
   611	
   612	We now show that the above examples from Theorem \ref{6-Waring} and Theorem \ref{thm:codim_three_P9} are not equivalent.
   613	\begin{proposition}
   614	Let $q\geq 4$, $\omega \in\bF_q\setminus\{0,1,2\}$ which is a square in $\bF_q$ and $\omega' \in \bF_q\setminus\{0,1,-1\}$. The subspaces
   615	\[ \mathcal{S}=\langle \mathbf{e}_1^{\otimes2},\mathbf{e}_2^{\otimes2},\mathbf{e}_3^{\otimes2},\mathbf{e}_4^{\otimes2},\mathbf{e}^{\otimes2},  (\omega\mathbf{e}_1+\mathbf{e}_2+\omega\mathbf{e}_3)^{\otimes2},(\sqrt{\omega}\mathbf{e}_1+\sqrt{\omega}\mathbf{e}_2+\sqrt{\omega^3}\mathbf{e}_4)^{\otimes2}  \rangle, \]
   616	and
   617	\[ \mathcal{S}'=\langle \mathbf{e}_1^{\otimes2},\mathbf{e}_2^{\otimes2},\mathbf{e}_3^{\otimes2},\mathbf{e}_4^{\otimes2},  (\mathbf{e}_2+\mathbf{e}_3+\mathbf{e}_4)^{\otimes2},\mathbf{v},\mathbf{w} \rangle, \]
   618	with $\mathbf{v}=(\mathbf{e}_1+\omega' \mathbf{e}_2+\omega' \mathbf{e}_3+\omega'^2\mathbf{e}_4)^{\otimes2}$ and $\mathbf{w}=(\mathbf{e}_1+\omega' \mathbf{e}_2+ \mathbf{e}_3+\omega' \mathbf{e}_4)^{\otimes2}$,
   619	are inequivalent.
   620	\end{proposition}
   621	\begin{proof}
   622	Recall that $\mathcal{S}$ and $\mathcal{S}'$ are equivalent if and only if the set of points $S$ defined by the vectors in $$\{\mathbf{e}_1,\mathbf{e}_2,\mathbf{e}_3,\mathbf{e}_4,\mathbf{e},  \omega\mathbf{e}_1+\mathbf{e}_2+\omega\mathbf{e}_3,\sqrt{\omega}\mathbf{e}_1+\sqrt{\omega}\mathbf{e}_2+\sqrt{\omega^3}\mathbf{e}_4\}$$ 
   623	and the set of points $S'$ defined by the vectors in 
   624	$$\{\mathbf{e}_1,\mathbf{e}_2,\mathbf{e}_3,\mathbf{e}_4,  \mathbf{e}_2+\mathbf{e}_3+\mathbf{e}_4,\mathbf{e}_1+\omega \mathbf{e}_2+\omega \mathbf{e}_3+\omega^2\mathbf{e}_4,\mathbf{e}_1+\omega \mathbf{e}_2+ \mathbf{e}_3+\omega \mathbf{e}_4\},$$ 
   625	are $\PGL(4,q)$-equivalent, i.e. $\phi(S)=S'$, for some $\phi \in \mathrm{PGL}(4,q)$.
   626	Now, observe that the number of planes of $\mathbb{P}^3_q$ meeting $S$ in exactly $4$ points is $4$, namely the planes $\pi_1:X_3=0$, $\pi_1:X_2=0$, $\pi_3:X_0-X_2=0$ and $\pi_4:X_0-X_1=0$. On the other hand, each of the planes $\pi'_1:X_0=0$, $\pi'_2:\omega' X_2-X_3=0$, $\pi'_3:X_1-X_3=0$, $\pi'_4:X_2-X_3=0$ and $\pi'_5:\omega' X_0-X_1=0$, meets
   627	$S'$ in exactly $4$ points. Therefore, such a $\phi \in \mathrm{PGL}(4,q)$ cannot exist and $\mathcal{S}$ and $\mathcal{S}'$ are inequivalent.
   628	\end{proof}
   629	In particular, the above result shows that the coefficient 
   630	$\eta_6(\mathbb{V}_{3,2}(\F_q))$ in the identifiable Waring polynomial of ${\mathcal{IW}}_{\mathbb{V}_{3,2}}(\F_q))$
   631	is $\geq 2$ when $q \geq 4$.
   632	
   633	
   634	\subsection{$7$-dimensional identifiable Waring subspaces}
   635	
   636	
   637	In this section we give a construction of $7$-dimensional identifiable Waring subspaces in $\PP_q^9$.
   638	The proof relies on the following lemma concerning the existence of $\bF_q$-rational points on an algebraic curve satisfying certain conditions. 
   639	
   640	For future reference we define the set 
   641	$$\mathcal B_*$$
   642	as the set of pairs $(q,\omega)$ satisfying one of the following conditions: $q\in \{4,5,7,8,9\}$ and $\omega$ a primitive element in $\bF_q$, $q=11$ and $\omega \in \{7,8\}$, or $q=13$ and $\omega\in \{2,7\}$.
   643	
   644	\begin{lemma}\label{lemma:cubic}
   645	Let $q$ be a prime power and let $\omega \in \mathbb{F}_q\setminus\{0,1,-1\}$.
   646	The curve $\mathcal{C}$ with affine equation
   647	\[ (\omega-1)^2X^2Y+\omega Y + (\omega-1)^2\omega XY^2+\omega^2 X+ (\omega-1)^2(\omega+1)XY=0  \]
   648	is absolutely irreducible.
   649	Also, it has no $\mathbb{F}_q$-rational point $(x,y)$ such that $x\ne-y$, $x\ne \frac{\omega}{\omega-1}$, $x\ne \frac{1}{\omega -1}$ and $1+x+\omega y \ne 0$ if and only if $(q,\omega)\in \mathcal B_*$.
   650	\end{lemma}
   651	\begin{proof}
   652	We start by proving that the planar cubic $\cC$ defined by the following polynomial
   653	\[ P(X,Y)=(\omega-1)^2X^2Y+\omega Y + (\omega-1)^2\omega XY^2+\omega^2 X+ (\omega-1)^2(\omega+1)XY \]
   654	is absolutely irreducible.
   655	Suppose, by way of contradiction, that $\cC$ is reducible over some field extension of $\bF_q$, then one of its component is a line $\ell$ having as point at infinity one of those of $\C$.
   656	The points at infinity of $\C$ are $(1:0:0)$, $(0:1:0)$ and $(-\omega:1:0)$.
   657	
   658	
   659	If $\ell$ has $(1:0:0)$ as its point at infinity, then $\ell$ has equation $Y=k$, for some $k \in \overline{\mathbb{F}}_q$, the algebraic closure of $\bF_q$.
   660	In particular, the polynomial
   661	\[ P(X,k)=k(\omega-1)^2 X^2+k\omega+k^2(\omega-1)^2\omega X+\omega^2 X+k(\omega-1)^2(\omega+1) X \]
   662	should be the zero polynomial. This is a contradiction since $\omega \ne 0$.
   663	
   664	If $\ell$ has $(0:1:0)$ as its point at infinity, then $\ell$  has equation $X=k$, for some $k \in \overline{\mathbb{F}}_q$.
   665	In particular, the polynomial
   666	\[ P(k,Y)=(\omega-1)^2k^2Y+\omega Y+\omega(\omega-1)^2 k Y^2+\omega^2 k+(\omega-1)^2(\omega+1)kY \]
   667	should be the zero polynomial. This is a contradiction since $\omega \ne 0$.
   668	
   669	If $\ell$ has $(-\omega:1:0)$ as its point at infinity, then $\ell$ has equation $X=-\omega Y+ k$, for some $k \in \overline{\mathbb{F}}_q$.
   670	In particular, the polynomial
   671	\[ P(-\omega Y +k,Y)= [-2(\omega-1)^2\omega+(\omega-1)^2\omega k-\omega (\omega-1)^2(\omega+1)]Y^2+\]
   672	\[ [(\omega-1)^2k^2+\omega-\omega^3+(\omega-1)^2(\omega+1)k]Y+\omega^2 k \]
   673	should be the zero polynomial.
   674	The constant term implies that $k=0$, and the coefficient of the term of degree one implies $\omega^3-\omega=0$. This is again a contradiction, since $\omega \notin \{0,\pm 1\}$.
   675	
   676	This shows that the cubic curve $\cC$ is absolutely irreducible.
   677	Note that the lines 
   678	$$
   679	\ell_1 \colon X+Y=0, \quad \ell_2 \colon X= \frac{\omega}{\omega-1}, \quad \ell_3 \colon X= \frac{1}{\omega -1}, \mbox{ and } \ell_4 \colon 1+X+\omega Y=0
   680	$$
   681	can share with the curve $\mathcal{C}$ at most three points.
   682	Therefore, denoting by $\mathcal{C}(\mathbb{F}_q)$ the set of $\mathbb{F}_q$-rational points of $\mathcal{C}$, if $|\mathcal{C}(\mathbb{F}_q)|\geq 14$, there exists at least one $\mathbb{F}_q$-rational affine point not lying on the lines $\ell_1$, $\ell_2$, $\ell_3$ and $\ell_4$. As $\mathcal{C}$ is a cubic curve, its genus is either $0$ or $1$, and by the Hasse-Weil bound we have
   683	\[ |\mathcal{C}(\mathbb{F}_q)|\geq q+1-2\sqrt{q}. \]
   684	Therefore, when $q\geq 23$, we have $|\mathcal{C}(\mathbb{F}_q)|\geq14$, and hence there exists an affine $\mathbb{F}_q$-rational point of $\mathcal{C}$, with affine coordinates $(x,y)$, satisfying $x\ne-y$, $x\ne \frac{\omega}{\omega-1}$, $x\ne \frac{1}{\omega -1}$ and $1+x+\omega y \ne 0$.
   685	The remaining cases $q\in \{16,17,19\}$ and the determination of the values of $\omega$ for $q \in \{4,5,7,8,9,11,13\}$ are easily verified by computer.
   686	\end{proof}
   687	
   688	
   689	
   690	\begin{theorem}\label{thm:codim_two_P9}
   691	Let $\omega$ be an element in $\bF_q\setminus\{0,1,-1\}$, and let $\mathcal S$ be the subspace defined in Theorem \ref{thm:codim_three_P9}.
   692	The subspace
   693	\[ \mathcal{S}'=\langle \mathcal{S},(\mathbf{e}_1+\mathbf{e}_3+\omega^2 \mathbf{e}_4)^{\otimes2}\rangle  \]
   694	is a $7$-dimensional identifiable Waring subspace of $\PP_q^9$ with respect to $\VV_{3,2}(\bF_q)$ if and only if $(q,\omega)\in \mathcal B_*$. In particular, $\mathcal{S}'$ is not an identifiable Waring subspace for $q\geq 16$.
   695	\end{theorem}
   696	
   697	\begin{proof}
   698	The nonzero vectors of $\mathcal{S}'$ can be represented by the matrices $M_\alpha$, with $\alpha=(a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h) \in \bF_q^8\setminus \{0\}$, where $M_\alpha$ is the matrix
   699	{\footnotesize{
   700	$$%\begin{equation}%\label{eqn:M}

exec
/bin/bash -lc "curl -Ls https://export.arxiv.org/e-print/2207.13456 | gzip -dc | nl -ba | sed -n '700,1220p'" in /home/gson/gvrepo/results/2207.13456
 succeeded in 0ms:
   700	$$%\begin{equation}%\label{eqn:M}
   701	\left(
   702	\begin{array}{cccc}
   703	 a+f+g+h & \omega f+\omega g & \omega f+g+h & \omega^2 f +\omega g+\omega^2 h  \\
   704	 \omega f+\omega g & b+e+\omega^2 f+\omega^2 g & e+\omega^2 f+\omega g & e+\omega^3 f +\omega^2 g \\
   705	 \omega f+g+h & e+\omega^2 f +\omega g & c+e+\omega^2 f+g+h & e+\omega^3 f +\omega g+\omega^2 h \\
   706	 \omega^2 f+\omega g+ \omega^2 h  & e+\omega^3 f+\omega^2 g & e+\omega^3 f+\omega g+\omega^2 h & d+e+\omega^4 f +\omega^2 g+\omega^4 h \\
   707	\end{array}
   708	\right).
   709	$$ %\end{equation} 
   710	}}
   711	One easily verifies that $\mathcal S'$ is a 7-dimensional subspace of $\PP^9$. In order to prove that $\mathcal S'$ is a Waring subspace, it suffices to show that the only matrices of rank one of the form $M_\alpha$ are obtained by setting all but one of the parameters $a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h$ equal to zero.
   712	
   713	By considering $M_{(1,4)(2,3)}$ we get
   714	\[ e(-1+(\omega-1)g)=-g(1+f(\omega-1))\omega^2(\omega -1). \]
   715	Note that $g\ne \frac{1}{\omega-1}$, otherwise by considering $M_{(2,3)(1,4)}=0$ we get $f=\frac{-1}{\omega-1}$, and from the condition $M_{(2,4)(1,4)}=0$ we would get $\omega(\omega-1)(\omega^2-\omega)=0$, which is not possible.
   716	Therefore, we have 
   717	$$e=\frac{-g(1+f(\omega-1))\omega^2(\omega -1)}{-1+(\omega-1)g}.$$
   718	Computing $M_{(2,3)(1,2)}=0$ we get
   719	\[ b(1+g+\omega f)=-\omega^2[f (1+g (\omega-1)^2)+\omega g]. \]
   720	Note that $1+g+\omega f\neq 0$, otherwise from $M_{(2,3)(1,2)}=0$ and from $M_{(1,3)(1,2)}=0$ we would get $f=\frac{-1}{\omega-1}$ and $a=-1$, from which it would follow that $M_{(2,4)(1,4)}=\omega^3(\omega-1)=0$, a contradiction. Hence, 
   721	$$b=-\omega^2\frac{f (1+g (\omega-1)^2)+\omega g}{1+g+\omega f}.$$ 
   722	From $M_{(1,2)(1,4)}=0$, we get either $f+g=0$ or 
   723	\[ a(g+\omega-\omega g )=-g(\omega-1)[f(\omega-1)+\omega]. \]
   724	If $g=-f$, then by $M_{(2,3)(2,3)}=0$ we get $f=0$ and one easily get $a=b=c=d=e=f=g=0$.
   725	Also, $g\neq \frac{\omega}{\omega-1}$, otherwise we would get a contradiction as before.
   726	Hence, we may assume that $f+g\ne 0$, $g\ne \frac{\omega}{\omega-1}$, $g\ne \frac{1}{\omega-1}$ and 
   727	\[ a=\frac{-g(\omega-1)[f(\omega-1)+\omega]}{g+\omega-\omega g}. \]
   728	The condition $M_{(2,3)(1,3)}=0$ implies
   729	\[ c= \frac{(\omega-1)[f(1+g(\omega-1)^2)+\omega g]}{f+g}, \]
   730	and the condition $M_{(1,4)(1,4)}=0$ implies either $g=-\omega-\omega f$ or
   731	\begin{eqnarray}\label{eqn:d}
   732	 d=-\frac{\omega^2(\omega-1)^2g[f(\omega-1)+\omega]}{-1+g(\omega-1)}.
   733	\end{eqnarray}
   734	If $g=-\omega-\omega f$, then $M_{(1,3)(1,3)}=(\omega-1)^2\ne 0$, again a contradiction.
   735	Assume $d$ satisfies Equation (\ref{eqn:d}). Then, as $f+g\ne 0$, $1+g+\omega f \ne 0$, $(\omega-1)g-\omega \ne 0$ and $1+g+\omega f\ne 0$, remaining minors of $M$ are either zero or scalar multiples of
   736	\[ (\omega-1)^2g^2f+\omega f+(\omega-1)^2\omega gf^2+\omega^2 g+ (\omega-1)^2(\omega+1) gf. \]
   737	It follows that the subspace $S'$ is a Waring subspace if and only if the affine cubic curve obtained by considering the above expression as a polynomial in the indeterminates $f$ and $g$ has no $\bF_q$-rational points 
   738	such that $g\ne-f$, $g\ne \frac{\omega}{\omega-1}$ and $g\ne \frac{1}{\omega -1}$.
   739	Applying Lemma \ref{lemma:cubic} concludes the proof.
   740	\end{proof}
   741	
   742	
   743	The identifiable Waring subspaces of codimension two in $\PP_q^9$ correspond to pencils of quadrics in $\bP_q^3$, with base consisting of eight points whose image under the Veronese map spans a seven-dimensional subspace of $\PP_q^9$. As far as we know there is no complete classification of pencils of quadrics in $\bP_q^3$. In what follows we will rely on the (partial) classification results from \cite{BruenHir}.
   744	We will also make use of the following lemma.
   745	
   746	\begin{lemma}\label{lem:int2cones}
   747	Let $C_1$ and $C_2$ be two irreducible quadratic cones in $\PP_q^3$, with $q\geq 53$. Then $C_1(\bF_q)$ does not meet $C_2(\bF_q)$ in eight points.
   748	\end{lemma}
   749	\begin{proof}
   750	Without loss of generality, we may assume that $C_1$ has equation $X_1X_3=X_0^2$ and $C_2$ has equation $f(X_0,X_1,X_2,X_3)=0$ with 
   751	$$f(X_0,X_1,X_2,X_3)=\sum_{i\leq j} f_{ij} X_iX_j\in \mathbb{F}_{q}[X_0,X_1,X_2,X_3].$$
   752	Observe that $C_1$ meets the plane at infinity $\pi_{\infty}\colon X_3=0$ in the line $\ell_{\infty}\colon X_0=X_3=0$ and so either $\ell_{\infty}$ is contained in $C_2$ or meets $C_2$ in at most two $\bF_q$-rational points.
   753	We may assume that $|\ell_{\infty} \cap C_2(\bF_q)|\leq 2$, otherwise $|C_1(\bF_q)\cap C_2(\bF_q)|\geq q+1>8$.
   754	An affine point $(x,y,z)$ is in $C_1 \cap C_2$ if and only if its coordinates satisfy the following system
   755	\begin{equation}\label{eq:coneint} \left\{ \begin{array}{ll} Y=X^2,\\ g(X,Y,Z)=0, \end{array}\right. \end{equation}
   756	where $g(X,Y,Z)=f(X,Y,Z,1)$.
   757	Let $h(X,Z)=g(X,X^2,Z)$, i.e.
   758	\[ h(X,Z)= (f_{00}+f_{13})X^2+f_{11}X^4+f_{22}Z^2+f_{01}X^3+f_{02}XZ+f_{12}X^2Z+f_{03}X+f_{23}Z+f_{33}, \]
   759	and observe that $h(X,Z)$ has degree at most $2$ in $Z$ and at most $4$ in $X$. 
   760	The number of solutions in $\mathbb{F}_q^3$ of System \eqref{eq:coneint} coincides with the number of $\mathbb{F}_q$-rational affine points of the planar curve $\mathcal{C}$ defined by $h(X,Z)=0$.
   761	Note that the number of points at infinity of $\mathcal{C}$ can be at most two (since $Y=X^2$ meets $\pi_{\infty}$ in $\ell_{\infty}$ which has at most two points in common with $C_2(\F_q)$). Hence if we prove that $\mathcal{C}$ has more than ten $\mathbb{F}_q$-rational points, then the size of $C_1(\bF_q)\cap C_2(\bF_q)$ is larger than eight.
   762	
   763	\bigskip
   764	
   765	First assume that $\mathcal{C}$ is absolutely irreducible. By applying the Hasse-Weil bound one gets that the number of $\mathbb{F}_q$-rational points of $\mathcal{C}$ is at least $q+1-6\sqrt{q}$ which is larger than $10$ because of the assumption on $q$.
   766	
   767	\bigskip
   768	
   769	Now, suppose that $\mathcal{C}$ is not absolutely irreducible. 
   770	
   771	\bigskip
   772	
   773	If $h(X,Z)$ splits over $\bF_q$ then $h(X,Z)$ has either a linear factor over $\bF_q$, an absolutely irreducible factor of degree two over $\bF_q$, or it is the product of $4$ linear factors over $\bF_{q^2}$ (two pairs of conjugates factors). This implies that the size of $\C(\bF_q)$ is either at least $q+1$, which is greater than $9$, or at most two. Together with the at most two points at infinity, the latter case implies that $C_1(\bF_q) \cap C_2(\bF_q)$ has size most $4$.
   774	
   775	\bigskip
   776	
   777	Finally, suppose that $h(X,Z)$ splits over some extension of $\bF_q$ but not over $\bF_q$.
   778	
   779	\bigskip
   780	
   781	If $f_{22}=0$ and $(f_{02},f_{12},f_{23})\ne (0,0,0)$, then $h(X,Z)$ is linear in $Z$, contradicting our assumption.
   782	If $(f_{22},f_{02},f_{12},f_{23})=(0,0,0,0)$, then $\mathcal{C}$ is the union of four lines (since $h(X,Z) \in \bF_q[X]$) through $(0:1:0)$. Since each of these lines has $(0:1:0)$ as its unique $\bF_q$-rational point, the 
   783	number of affine $\bF_q$-rational points on $\cC$ is one. Therefore, also the
   784	number of affine points in $C_1(\bF_q)\cap C_2(\bF_q)$ is one.
   785	Note that in this case the line $\ell=C_1 \cap \pi_{\infty}$ meets $C_2$ at the point $(0:0:1:0)$, so that $C_1(\bF_q)\cap C_2(\bF_q)$ has size two.
   786	
   787	\bigskip
   788	
   789	If $f_{22}\ne 0$ then without loss of generality we may assume $f_{22}=1$. Then 
   790	$$h(X,Z)=(Z-\alpha(X))(Z-\beta(X)),$$
   791	with
   792	$$\alpha(X)=a_2X^2+a_1X+a_0\notin\mathbb{F}_q[X]$$ 
   793	and 
   794	$$\beta(X)=a_2^qX^2+a_1^qX+a_0^q,$$ 
   795	for some $a_0,a_2,a_2\in \bF_{q^2}[X]$.
   796	The point $(x,z)$ is an $\mathbb{F}_q$-rational point of $\mathcal{C}$ if and only if it is an $\mathbb{F}_q$-rational point of
   797	\[ \left\{ \begin{array}{ll} Z=\alpha(X), \\ Z=\beta(X), \end{array}\right. \]
   798	which can have at most four $\bF_q$-rational points.
   799	Together with the points at infinity, $C_1(\bF_q) \cap C_2(\bF_q)$ can have at most $6$ points.
   800	\end{proof}
   801	
   802	
   803	\begin{theorem}\label{th:class7}
   804	For $q\geq 53$ there are no identifiable Waring subspaces of codimension two in $\PP_q^9$ with respect to $\VV_{3,2}(\bF_q)$, i.e. 
   805	$$\eta_7(\VV_{3,2}(\bF_{q\geq 53}))=0.$$
   806	Furthermore, an identifiable Waring subspace of codimension two in $\PP_q^9$  with respect to $\VV_{3,2}(\bF_q)$, for $q\leq 13$, is spanned by the images under $\nu_2$ of one the following curves of $\PP_q^3$:
   807	\begin{enumerate}
   808	    \item four distinct lines for $q=2$;
   809	    \item two skew lines over $\bF_q$ and two conjugate lines over $\bF_{q^2}$ for $q=3$;
   810	    \item two disjoint conics over $\bF_q$ for $q=3$;
   811	    \item two conics intersecting in two points for $q=4$;
   812	    \item an elliptic curve for $q\in \{ 4,5,7,8,9,11,13 \}$;
   813	    \item an absolutely irreducible rational curve (which is not a twisted cubic and which is a base of a linear system of quadrics of dimension two) for $q=7$.
   814	\end{enumerate}
   815	Moreover, each of these cases occurs and gives at least one example of an identifiable Waring subspace. 
   816	\end{theorem}
   817	
   818	\begin{proof}
   819	Recall that identifiable Waring subspaces of dimension $7$ correspond to pencils of quadrics whose base consists of exactly $8$ points which is not contained in any net of quadrics of $\PP^3$. 
   820	The pencil of quadrics defined by the quadratic forms $f$ and $g$ is denoted by $\cP(f,g)$.
   821	We can divide the analysis of pencils of quadrics into the following cases:
   822	\begin{enumerate}[$(i)$]
   823	\item the pencil contains a non-singular quadric;
   824	\item the pencil consists of singular quadrics and at least two quadratic cones;
   825	\item the pencil consists of singular quadrics and one quadratic cone;
   826	\item the pencil consists of reducible quadrics.
   827	\end{enumerate}
   828	In case $(i)$, by \cite[Sections 4 and 8]{BruenHir}, the base curve $\C$ of the pencil can be one of the following curves (for convenience of the reader, we follow the numbering from \cite{BruenHir}). By the above, we may restrict ourselves to the case where the number of $\bF_q$-rational points of $\cC$ can be even.
   829	
   830	[1(a)] $\C$ is the union of four distinct lines over $\bF_q$, $|\C(\bF_q)|=4q$.
   831	An example is given by $\cP(X_0X_2,X_1X_3)$, and for $q=2$ we obtain a codimension two identifiable Waring subspace.
   832	
   833	[1(b)] $\C$ is the union of two distinct skew lines over $\bF_q$ and two conjugate lines over $\bF_{q^2}$, $|\C(\bF_q)|=2q+2$. An example is given by 
   834	$$\cP(X_0X_3-X_1X_2,X_0X_2+b X_1 X_2 +X_1 X_3),$$
   835	and this gives an identifiable Waring subspace for $q=3$.
   836	
   837	[3(a)] $\C$ is the union of two disjoint conics over $\bF_q$, $|\C(\bF_q)|=2q+2$.
   838	An example is given by 
   839	$$\cP(X_0X_1, X_0^2+X_1^2+X_2^2+b X_2X_3+X_3^2),$$
   840	where $b \in \mathbb{F}_q$ such that $x^2+bx+1$ is irreducible over $\mathbb{F}_q$ $b \in \mathbb{F}_q$ such that $x^2+bx+1$ is irreducible over $\mathbb{F}_q$. For $q$ odd there is another (inequivalent) example given by 
   841	$$\cP(X_0X_1,X_0^2+\nu X_1^2+X_2^2+b X_2X_3+X_3^2),$$ 
   842	where  and $\nu$ is a non-square in $\mathbb{F}_q$. Both pencils give identifiable Waring subspaces for $q=3$, which are inequivalent.
   843	
   844	[3(c)] $\C$ is the union of two conics over $\bF_q$ with two points in common, $|\C(\bF_q)|=2q$.
   845	An example is given by 
   846	$$\cP(X_0X_1,X_0^2+X_1^2+ X_2X_3),$$  
   847	and for $q$ odd, there is another inequivalent example given by 
   848	$$\cP(X_0X_1,X_0^2+\nu X_1^2+ X_2X_3),$$ 
   849	with $\nu$ a non-square in $\mathbb{F}_q$.
   850	These pencils give inequivalent identifiable Waring subspaces for $q=4$.
   851	
   852	
   853	[4(a)] $\C$ is the union of a twisted cubic and a bisecant, $|\C(\bF_q)|=2q$.
   854	There is unique such pencil. An example is given by $\cP(X_0X_2-X_1^2,X_1 X_3-X_2^2)$, and this pencil induces an identifiable Waring subspace for $q=4$.
   855	
   856	[4(c)] $\C$ is the union of a twisted cubic and an imagining chord, $|\C(\bF_q)|=2q+2$.
   857	Again there is a unique such pencil. An example is given by
   858	$\cP(X_0X_3-X_1 X_2+b X_1 X_3-bX_2^2,X_0 X_2 -X_1^2-X_1X_3+X_2^2)$, with  $b \in \mathbb{F}_q$ such that $x^2+bx+1$ is irreducible over $\mathbb{F}_q$. This pencil gives an identifiable Waring subspace for $q=3$.
   859	
   860	[5(a)] $\C$ is an irreducible elliptic curve, $(\sqrt{q}-1)^2\leq |\C(\bF_q)|\leq (\sqrt{q}+1)^2$.
   861	It is well-known that elliptic curves are the intersection of two quadrics of $\PP^3$ and hence the subspaces spanned by the images of the $\bF_q$-rational points are identifiable Waring subspaces of codimension two in $\PP^9$. 
   862	Moreover, the existence of such subspaces is guaranteed by Theorem \ref{thm:codim_two_P9}.
   863	
   864	[5(b)] $\C$ is an irreducible rational curve, $|\C(\bF_q)|= q+1$. Since the twisted cubic is the base curve of a linear system of quadrics in $\PP^3$ of dimension three for any $q$; see \cite[Lemma 21.1.6]{Hir3}, this case does not lead to an identifiable Waring subspace. 
   865	
   866	Now suppose $q\geq 53$. Clearly, as we need $8$ points, we can now analyze the case when the size of the intersections of the two quadrics is less than $q+1$. This means that we only need to consider the cases $(ii)$, $(iii)$, and $(iv)$.
   867	The case of the intersection of two quadratic cones is taken care of by Lemma \ref{lem:int2cones}.
   868	Consider now the intersection between a pair of planes $\pi_1 \cup \pi_2$ and a cone $C$ such that $|C\cap (\pi_1\cup\pi_2)|<q+1$. Then $|C\cap (\pi_1\cup\pi_2)|\leq 1$, since a plane $\pi$ meets a cone in either zero points, one point (the vertex of the cone) or $q+1$ points.
   869	As we need $8$ points, this case does not give rise to any identifiable Waring subspaces.
   870	If the two quadrics are two pairs of planes, then their intersection is at least $q+1$ points. Again, this case does not give rise to any identifiable Waring subspaces.
   871	\end{proof}
   872	
   873	
   874	\begin{remark}
   875	We have verified computationally, that there are no identifiable Waring subspaces of codimension two in $\PP_{16}^9$.
   876	\end{remark}
   877	
   878	\begin{corollary}
   879	The coefficient $\eta_7(\mathbb{V}_{3,2}(\F_q))$ of the identifiable Waring polynomial of $\mathbb{V}_{3,2}(\F_q)$ is: 
   880	\begin{itemize}
   881	\item one if $q =2$;
   882	\item three if $q=3$;
   883	\item at least $2$ if $q=4$;
   884	\item at least one if $q\in \{5,7,8,9,11,13\}$.
   885	\end{itemize}
   886	\end{corollary}
   887	\begin{proof}
   888	It is clear that two identifiable Waring subspaces of codimension two in $\PP_q^9$ obtained by curves from two different cases (1)-(5) of Theorem \ref{th:class7} are not equivalent.
   889	By \cite[Theorem 4.4]{BruenHir}, the number of equivalence classes arising from the examples in (1),(2) and (4) of Theorem \ref{th:class7} is one, whereas the number of equivalence classes arising from the examples in (3) of Theorem \ref{th:class7} is two.
   890	The assertion is then proved.
   891	\end{proof}
