   893	
   894	
   895	\subsection{Identifiable Waring hyperplanes}
   896	Since the hyperplanes sections of $\bP_q^9$ correspond to quadrics of $\PP_q^3$, we have the following.
   897	
   898	\begin{theorem}\label{th:classhyper3}
   899	The identifiable Waring hyperplanes of $\PP_q^9$ exist only for $q=2$ and they are the span of the images under $\nu_2$ of hyperbolic quadrics in $\PP_q^3$. In particular, $\eta_8(\VV_{3,2}(\bF_2))=1$ and $\eta_8(\VV_{3,2}(\bF_{q>2}))=0$.
   900	\end{theorem}
   901	\begin{proof}
   902	In order to obtain an identifiable Waring hyperplane, one needs a quadric of $\bP_q^3$ consisting of exactly nine points, with the property that its image under the Veronese map spans a hyperplane. A quadric in $\bP_q^3$ is one of the following:\begin{itemize}
   903	  \item a hyperbolic quadric (i.e. $(q+1)^2$ points);
   904	  \item an elliptic quadric (i.e. $q^2+1$ points);
   905	  \item an irreducible quadratic cone (i.e. $q^2+q+1$ points);
   906	  \item a pair of conjugate planes over $\bF_{q^2}$ (i.e. $q+1$ points)
   907	  \item two distinct planes (i.e. $2q^2+q+1$ points);
   908	  \item one plane (i.e. $q^2+q+1$ points).
   909	\end{itemize}
   910	
   911	The only possibility is a hyperbolic quadric in $\bP_2^3$, and there is a unique such quadric up to projective equivalence.
   912	\end{proof}
   913	
   914	\section{Waring polynomials for elliptic and hyperbolic quadrics in $\bP^3(\bF_q)$}
   915	
   916	In this section we collect some of the well-known properties of the elliptic and hyperbolic quadrics in $\bP(\bF_q)$ in terms of their Waring polynomials.
   917	
   918	
   919	Let $\cX=\mathcal{E}$ be an elliptic quadric in $\bP^3_q$. The points on $\mathcal{E}(\bF_q)$ are Waring subspaces, and the group of the quadric acts transitively on them. When $q=2$, the quadric $\mathcal{E}(\bF_q)$ consists of 5 points.
   920	For any external point $P$ of the quadric there exist two tangent planes and one $3$-secant plane (where the three points of intersection are not collinear). Then if $P$ is not the nuclei of the conic contained in the secant plane it will have $\mathcal{E}$-rank two, otherwise it will have $\mathcal{E}$-rank $3$.
   921	A line $\ell$ is a Waring subspace with respect to $\mathcal{E}$ if and only if $|\ell \cap \mathcal{E}(\bF_q)|=2$ and the other lines are neither Waring subspaces nor Waring identifiable subspaces with respect to $\mathcal{E}$, unless $q=2$, in which case each external line is Waring identifiable, since it is contained in a unique plane of $\bP_2^3$ meeting $\mathcal E$ in a non-degenerate conic.
   922	As the planes of $\PP_q^3$ meets $\mathcal{E}(\bF_q)$ in either an irreducible conic or in a point, when $q\geq3$ then the planes meeting $\mathcal{E}(\bF_q)$ in more than one point are Waring subspaces but not Waring identifiable subspaces, whereas when $q=2$ the Waring planes are also identifiable Waring planes. The tangent planes are neither Waring subspace nor Waring identifiable subspace with respect to $\mathcal{E}$.
   923	Therefore,
   924	if $q\ne 2$, then
   925	\[ \mathcal{W}_{\mathcal{E}}(\mathbb{F}_q)=1+X+X^2,\,\,\mathcal{WI}_{\mathcal{E}}(\mathbb{F}_q)=1+X,\, \mathcal{IW}_{\mathcal{E}}(\mathbb{F}_q)=1+X, \]
   926	whereas if $q=2$
   927	\[ \mathcal{W}_{\mathcal{E}}(\mathbb{F}_2)=1+X+X^2,\,\,\mathcal{WI}_{\mathcal{E}}(\mathbb{F}_2)=3+2X+X^2,\, \mathcal{IW}_{\mathcal{E}}(\mathbb{F}_2)=1+X+X^2. \]
   928	When $\cX=\mathcal{H}$ is a hyperbolic quadric, then the points of $\mathcal{H}(\bF_q)$ are Waring subspaces, through each external point there are at least two $2$-secant lines, hence they are not Waring identifiable. 
   929	The only Waring lines are the $2$-secant lines (which are Waring identifiable) and the $(q+1)$-secant lines of $\mathcal{H}(\bF_q)$ (which are not Waring identifiable).
   930	Identifiable Waring planes exist if and only if $q=2$ and are those meeting $\mathcal{H}(\bF_q)$ in an irreducible conic.
   931	When $q\geq 3$, there do not exist identifiable Waring planes but there are two types of Waring planes: those meeting $\mathcal{H}(\bF_q)$ in an irreducible conic and those meeting $\mathcal{H}(\bF_q)$ is two lines. 
   932	These two type of planes are inequivalent. 
   933	Therefore, 
   934	if $q\ne 2$, then
   935	\[ \mathcal{W}_{\mathcal{H}}(\mathbb{F}_q)=1+2X+2X^2,\,\,\mathcal{WI}_{\mathcal{H}}(\mathbb{F}_q)=1+X,\, \mathcal{IW}_{\mathcal{H}}(\mathbb{F}_q)=1+X, \]
   936	whereas if $q=2$
   937	\[ \mathcal{W}_{\mathcal{H}}(\mathbb{F}_2)=1+2X+2X^2,\,\,\mathcal{WI}_{\mathcal{H}}(\mathbb{F}_2)=1+X+X^2,\, \mathcal{IW}_{\mathcal{H}}(\mathbb{F}_2)=1+X+X^2. \]
   938	
   939	\section{Conclusions}
   940	
   941	In this paper we introduced the concepts of (identifiable) Waring subspaces and Waring identifiable subspaces with respect to an algebraic variety. The number of projectively inequivalent such subspaces is captured with the relevant Waring polynomials.
   942	We have seen how examples easily carry out when considering conics or quadrics over finite fields. Most of the paper is devoted to study the case when the involved algebraic variety is the Veronese variety, motivated by the connection with the theory of symmetric tensors and in particular with the Waring identifiability of symmetric tensors.
   943	In particular, we have given examples of Waring subspaces with respect to the quadric Veronese variety and we have obtained a complete classification in $\PP^5$. Constructions and classification results are also obtained in $\PP^{9}$, but in this case a complete classification seems more difficult and the subject of possible future work. 
   944	
   945	Another interesting problem is whether or not the Waring identifiability cases of \cite[Section 1]{AGMO} carry through over finite fields.
   946	Since over finite fields the notion of \emph{generic form}/\emph{generic point} cannot be defined, our aim will be to find the parameters $(r,n,d_1,\ldots,d_r,k)$ for which there exists at least one $r$-tuple of forms in $\mathbb{F}_q[x_1,\ldots,x_n]_{d_1}\times\mathbb{F}_q[x_1,\ldots,x_n]_{d_r}$ which is Waring identifiable.
   947	We remark that there are examples which still hold over finite fields, for instance the following one which is the one shown by Sylvester in \cite{Sy} over the complex field.
   948	
   949	\begin{proposition}\label{prop:fin}
   950	Let $t$ be a positive integer and $q\geq 2t+1$. Every point of $\PP^{2t+1}$ having $\VV_{1,2t+1}$-rank $t+1$ is Waring identifiable.
   951	\end{proposition}
   952	\begin{proof}
   953	As $\VV_{1,2t+1}$ is a normal rational curve of $\PP^{2t+1}$ and hence an arc in $\PP^{2t+1}$, any two $(t+1)$-dimensional Waring subspaces with respect to $\VV_{1,2t+1}$ are disjoint or meet in at least one point on $\VV_{1,2t+1}$.
   954	Therefore, through any point $P$ of $\PP^{2t+1}$ having $\VV_{1,2t+1}$-rank $t+1$ there exists only one $(t+1)$-dimensional Waring subspace with respect to $\VV_{1,2t+1}$ and hence $P$ is Waring identifiable.
   955	\end{proof}
   956	
   957	The following corollary extends the known examples of Waring identifiability of \cite[Section 1]{AGMO}.
   958	
   959	\begin{corollary}
   960	If $q\geq 2t+1$, there exist forms in $\mathbb{F}_q[x_1,x_2]_{2t+1}$ of rank $t+1$ which are Waring identifiable.
   961	\end{corollary}
   962	
   963	Note that the proof of Proposition \ref{prop:fin} may be adapted to any arc in a projective space of odd dimension. 
   964	An example of such an arc is the following: 
   965	let $h$ and $e$ two positive integers with $\gcd(h,e)=1$, let $q=2^h$ and let $\sigma\colon x \in \bF_q\mapsto x^{2^e}$ then the set
   966	\[ \{(1,t,t^\sigma,t^{\sigma+1}) \colon t \in \bF_q\} \cup \{(0,0,0,1)\} \]
   967	is an arc of $q+1$ points in $\PP^3$ known as the \emph{Segre arc}, \cite{Segre} and see also \cite{BL}.
   968	
